1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,990
This is a full walkthrough of Thenar, recorded live

2
00:00:03,020 --> 00:00:07,064
against Monad testnet. Every transaction you'll see is real.

3
00:00:07,744 --> 00:00:10,628
Robots learn from human demonstrations, and the people

4
00:00:10,658 --> 00:00:13,298
who record them are rarely paid, and never own the

5
00:00:13,328 --> 00:00:16,154
result. Thenar is for them.

6
00:00:16,184 --> 00:00:19,835
The front page shows Monad testnet live, every block

7
00:00:19,865 --> 00:00:21,842
timed from proposed to final.

8
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:28,573
First, the other side of the market:

9
00:00:28,603 --> 00:00:31,557
a robotics lab that needs demonstrations.

10
00:00:31,587 --> 00:00:34,460
Its budget lives in a Privy server wallet, under a

11
00:00:34,490 --> 00:00:37,153
policy that allows exactly one action:

12
00:00:37,183 --> 00:00:40,101
funding a task on Thenar's contract.

13
00:00:40,281 --> 00:00:43,072
Here it posts a bounty for the toothpaste task.

14
00:00:43,102 --> 00:00:45,975
Privy signs it, and Monad makes it final.

15
00:00:46,005 --> 00:00:49,364
Asked to send money anywhere else, Privy refuses to sign.

16
00:00:50,044 --> 00:00:53,195
An operator arrives. In production they sign in by email

17
00:00:53,225 --> 00:00:55,947
through Privy; here a test wallet is injected.

18
00:00:55,977 --> 00:00:57,966
Each step is read from the chain.

19
00:00:58,646 --> 00:01:01,452
Next, a passkey. Its public key goes into

20
00:01:01,482 --> 00:01:05,472
PasskeyRegistry, and Monad's P-256 precompile checks

21
00:01:05,502 --> 00:01:09,447
every signature, so a paid run always comes from a person.

22
00:01:10,127 --> 00:01:13,905
With Mera, the passkey's PRF derives a second key:

23
00:01:13,935 --> 00:01:16,524
the one a physical SO-101 obeys.

24
00:01:16,554 --> 00:01:19,953
No seed phrase, and nothing leaves the device.

25
00:01:20,633 --> 00:01:22,658
Now the lab's task at the station.

26
00:01:22,688 --> 00:01:26,687
It can be driven by keyboard, by a leader arm over USB,

27
00:01:26,717 --> 00:01:30,704
or in a headset. Here it's WebXR, with Meta's emulator

28
00:01:30,734 --> 00:01:32,642
standing in for a Quest 3S.

29
00:01:33,322 --> 00:01:36,496
Press A to begin. Squeeze to take hold of the arm, and

30
00:01:36,526 --> 00:01:38,528
the tool follows the controller millimetre for

31
00:01:38,558 --> 00:01:41,941
millimetre. The trigger closes the jaws on the payload.

32
00:01:42,121 --> 00:01:45,771
Lift it, carry it round, and lower it into the ring.

33
00:01:45,801 --> 00:01:47,989
Every sample of the arm is recorded, because the

34
00:01:48,019 --> 00:01:51,250
verifier scores the motion, not just where it ends.

35
00:02:03,322 --> 00:02:06,171
In tolerance: placement, smoothness and efficiency,

36
00:02:06,201 --> 00:02:09,572
scored in the page from the same samples the verifier will check.

37
00:02:10,252 --> 00:02:13,333
Before submitting, the page shows the gas:

38
00:02:13,363 --> 00:02:16,886
Monad charges the limit, not the gas used.

39
00:02:16,916 --> 00:02:20,601
Submit: the verifier re-scores the samples and signs,

40
00:02:20,631 --> 00:02:24,675
and one transaction records the run and pays the operator.

41
00:02:25,355 --> 00:02:29,920
Executed 1.09 s after the send, and final at 1.21 s, on

42
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:33,323
Monad testnet, measured in this browser.

43
00:02:33,503 --> 00:02:35,296
The details show each step:

44
00:02:35,326 --> 00:02:38,628
the node saw it in its pool, the receipt came back, then

45
00:02:38,658 --> 00:02:41,774
its block reached Monad's finalized tag, with the hash

46
00:02:41,804 --> 00:02:45,651
checked. The page also prices the same run on Ethereum.

47
00:02:46,331 --> 00:02:49,389
And the run issues corpus shares to the operator, so

48
00:02:49,419 --> 00:02:52,024
they own a part of the dataset they helped build.

49
00:02:52,704 --> 00:02:56,728
The run is now data. The corpus page shows each episode

50
00:02:56,758 --> 00:03:00,104
drawn from the samples its hash commits to, a Merkle

51
00:03:00,134 --> 00:03:03,288
root over the paid set, and two ways to buy:

52
00:03:03,318 --> 00:03:08,381
a subscription for every task, or one task over x402.

53
00:03:09,061 --> 00:03:11,910
Here's the buyer: an AI agent with a wallet and no

54
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:15,593
account. It asks for the lab's corpus, gets a 402 with

55
00:03:15,623 --> 00:03:19,342
the terms, and signs a one-cent USDC authorisation.

56
00:03:19,372 --> 00:03:23,869
Monad's facilitator settles it, and the file comes back.

57
00:03:23,899 --> 00:03:26,957
The agent hashes the file and checks it against the sale

58
00:03:26,987 --> 00:03:28,407
logged on chain.

59
00:03:29,087 --> 00:03:32,308
The agents page states the terms the 402 gives, where

60
00:03:32,338 --> 00:03:35,291
the money goes, and every sale in the log.

61
00:03:39,087 --> 00:03:41,875
Thenar's server is the party being checked, so a

62
00:03:41,905 --> 00:03:44,653
Chainlink CRE workflow audits it.

63
00:03:44,683 --> 00:03:48,113
It fetches the episode hashes, rebuilds each task's

64
00:03:48,143 --> 00:03:51,505
Merkle root itself, reads what the verifier committed on

65
00:03:51,535 --> 00:03:54,696
Monad, and writes a verdict per task to the CorpusAudit

66
00:03:54,726 --> 00:03:58,469
contract. This run uses the CRE simulator, with a real

67
00:03:58,499 --> 00:04:00,059
broadcast to Monad.

68
00:04:00,739 --> 00:04:03,360
Its verdict for the lab's task is uncommitted:

69
00:04:03,390 --> 00:04:05,778
the episodes are served, but the verifier hasn't

70
00:04:05,808 --> 00:04:07,861
committed their root yet.

71
00:04:07,891 --> 00:04:11,262
CRE says so on chain, and the corpus page shows it

72
00:04:11,292 --> 00:04:14,048
beside the data, for any buyer to read.

73
00:04:14,728 --> 00:04:17,647
The leaderboard ranks operators by what they've earned,

74
00:04:17,677 --> 00:04:21,246
read back from the trajectory ledger, and agents by what

75
00:04:21,276 --> 00:04:22,278
they've bought.

76
00:04:23,228 --> 00:04:25,052
Finally, the network page.

77
00:04:25,082 --> 00:04:28,246
It reads Monad and Ethereum side by side:

78
00:04:28,276 --> 00:04:32,478
block time, time to final, gas price, and how each one

79
00:04:32,508 --> 00:04:35,957
charges gas. It checks a P-256 signature through the

80
00:04:35,987 --> 00:04:39,230
precompile live, and shows the staking epoch.

81
00:04:39,410 --> 00:04:42,213
That's Thenar: identity, provenance, payment and

82
00:04:42,243 --> 00:04:44,546
ownership, each enforced on Monad.

